WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Methods of Counting Defect Opportunities

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Hello, Mike Harry back with you again. Where we’re exploring defect opportunities.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.166
And we’re going to take a look at a few more examples here. Let's move forward here.

4
00:00:13.166 --> 00:00:18.832
What we’re looking at here, is a simulation in a bank.

5
00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:22.833
Of people coming and going to the different tellers.

6
00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:26.799
And so, let's take an idea here.

7
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.633
And that idea is this, every person is an opportunity.

8
00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:36.499
So when we ask ourselves the following question,

9
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.733
when this person, the customer, approaches this teller,

10
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:47.499
what must go right? We see, the inverse of that is what could go wrong?

11
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:51.500
And let's say that we identify twenty-five things.

12
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.600
And through the use of customer satisfaction surveys,

13
00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:02.633
or direct observation through cameras, and interviews, etc.,

14
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:06.933
we can establish the number of things that went wrong, i.e. defects.

15
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:13.533
And of course the opportunity count would be the number of individuals that actually come in

16
00:01:13.533 --> 00:01:19.366
and arrive at a tellers desk. That's one example.

17
00:01:19.366 --> 00:01:23.666
Let’s take a look at another.

18
00:01:23.666 --> 00:01:31.032
In this case produce testing. What they've done is there testing lettuce.

19
00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:34.699
And you can see them there doing that.

20
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.700
There's the gentleman with the bag of lettuce here, that draws a sample.

21
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:46.333
To look for bacteriological growth, in that sample.

22
00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:52.099
There's the little sample right there, of which he's observing.

23
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:57.966
And there might be, let's say, several different strains of bacteria they’re looking for.

24
00:01:57.966 --> 00:02:04.399
So, every one of those strains is an opportunity, per sample.

25
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.800
Just like a solder joint, that little piece right there is equivalent to a solder joint.

26
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.300
Has defect opportunities.

27
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:21.333
And of course, a classic one, machining.

28
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:24.599
To where we're looking at

29
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:30.033
a tool coming in here to drill a hole.

30
00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:34.033
Well, the hole has to be round.

31
00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:39.133
See, concentricity. That's an opportunity. That's a CTQ.

32
00:02:39.133 --> 00:02:43.133
So, in general we can draw this conclusion,

33
00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:49.099
any CTQ, anything that is critical to quality.

34
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.366
See, the solder joint is critical to value,

35
00:02:52.366 --> 00:02:55.866
but one of the six categories is critical to quality.

36
00:02:55.866 --> 00:03:00.499
Each defect category is a critical to quality characteristic.

37
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.900
And some of those defect categories are continuous by nature

38
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.900
and others are discrete, pass-fail.

39
00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:11.566
But at the bottom line, at the end of the day, we rate them all as pass-fail,

40
00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:14.832
for purposes of defect counting.

41
00:03:14.833 --> 00:03:19.733
Well in this case, concentricity here, is a continuous measure.

42
00:03:19.733 --> 00:03:24.033
And by measuring it, getting the

43
00:03:24.033 --> 00:03:27.599
specific measured value, and comparing it to the standard.

44
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:34.600
If it's within the spec limits, it passes. If it don't, it fails. So again, zero-one.

45
00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:41.566
And there we see yet more machining going on, a different type of machining operation.

46
00:03:41.566 --> 00:03:47.399
So, all of those have CTQ’s.

47
00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.666
Like what is going on right here.

48
00:03:50.666 --> 00:03:54.666
There's supposed to be a radius, maybe, on the edge of the material.

49
00:03:54.666 --> 00:03:58.666
And he’s putting that radius on there.

50
00:03:58.666 --> 00:04:02.666
That's a critical to quality characteristic.

51
00:04:02.666 --> 00:04:08.699
The same thing back here with this machine. Here, we’ll let it run.

52
00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:15.366
As it’s operating away, maybe it's milling a slot through here.

53
00:04:15.366 --> 00:04:20.999
And the slot should be of a certain width and certain depth. Well, there’s two different CTQ’s,

54
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.733
two critical to quality characteristics, two defect opportunities.

55
00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:32.733
So, with that thought, once again, we can see how it can be applied

56
00:04:32.733 --> 00:04:36.366
in the industrial world as well as the commercial world.

57
00:04:36.366 --> 00:04:39.432
And remember, it's very, very simple.

58
00:04:39.433 --> 00:04:46.399
All we have to do. Define the product.

59
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:51.900
Create a hierarchical tree of what comprises that product.

60
00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:57.233
Down at the bottom of the tree is a product element, like a solder joint.

61
00:04:57.233 --> 00:05:03.066
That element has k number of defect categories.

62
00:05:03.066 --> 00:05:07.966
Every defect category constitutes an opportunity.

63
00:05:07.966 --> 00:05:14.966
And from there, we can compute the Sigma, or the yield, or any other number of metrics.

64
00:05:14.966 --> 00:05:20.766
But at the end of the day we’re looking, where Six Sigma is concerned, at defects

65
00:05:20.766 --> 00:05:24.766
per opportunity, defects per million opportunities.

66
00:05:24.766 --> 00:05:30.899
And with that, that concludes our discussion on defect opportunities.

67
00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:35.900
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
